# Release checklist

Copy per release. Each line needs evidence (command and result, link, or
reviewer) before it is ticked. Publication steps belong to the approved
release operator.

## Prepare

- [ ] Release branch or commit identified: `<sha>` on `<branch>`
- [ ] All changes intended for this release are merged; nothing unintended is
- [ ] Next version decided from the SemVer rules: `<old> -> <new>` (reason)
- [ ] Breaking changes, deprecations and removals listed with migration notes
- [ ] Changelog `Unreleased` section edited for users and renamed to
      `[<new>] - YYYY-MM-DD`; comparison links updated
- [ ] Version updated in every version file (bump tool dry run reviewed)
- [ ] Documentation for new or changed behavior merged

## Verify the candidate

- [ ] Completion gate passed on the release commit: `<command>` -> `<result>`
- [ ] Distributions built from a clean checkout with the locked toolchain
- [ ] Artifact contents inspected: no secrets, private files, caches or
      build debris; license and notices included
- [ ] Installed from the built artifact outside the source tree and smoke
      tested on each supported runtime
- [ ] Checksums generated for exactly the files to publish
- [ ] Dependency and vulnerability scan reviewed for shipped dependencies

## Tag and publish (operator)

- [ ] Signed annotated tag `v<new>` created on the release commit and verified
      locally (`git tag -v v<new>`)
- [ ] Tag pushed individually (not `--tags`)
- [ ] Release workflow run: `<link>`; build, attestation and publish jobs green
- [ ] Release environment approval recorded: `<reviewer>`
- [ ] Release notes match the changelog section
- [ ] Attestation verified for each published file
      (`gh attestation verify <file> --repo <owner>/<repo>`)
- [ ] Package registry publication confirmed (if applicable)

## After release

- [ ] New empty `Unreleased` section added to the changelog
- [ ] Announcement or upgrade notes published where users look
- [ ] Previous supported version and rollback path documented
- [ ] Follow-up issues filed for deferred work and known limitations

## Abort or roll back

- Stop condition reached (failed gate, wrong contents, failed attestation):
  do not publish; fix forward with a new version.
- Published release is wrong: never move or recreate the tag. Mark the release
  and package version as yanked or deprecated, publish a fixed version, and
  record the incident.
